<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Determining the maximum number of bags a farmer can buy within a budget</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>{@fname@}, a local farmer, has a total budget of <b>KSh {@budget@}</b> to buy {@item@}. Transporting the goods to the farm costs a flat rate of <b>KSh {@transport@}</b>, regardless of the amount bought. Each bag costs <b>KSh {@cost@}</b>.</p>

<p><b>(a)</b> If \(n\) represents the number of bags {@fname@} can buy, write a simple linear inequality to represent this situation.</p>
<p><i>(Use the variable \(n\). Use <b>&lt;=</b> for \(\le\))</i></p>
<p>Inequality: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p><b>(b)</b> Solve the inequality to determine the <b>maximum number of whole bags</b> the farmer can buy.</p>
<p>Max bags: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part (a): Forming the Inequality</b></p>
<p>The total cost consists of the flat transport fee plus the cost of the bags. Since each bag costs KSh {@cost@}, the cost of \(n\) bags is \({@cost@}n\).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \text{Total Cost} = {@transport@} + {@cost@}n \)
</p>
<p>The farmer cannot spend more than their budget of KSh {@budget@}. Therefore, we combine these to write the inequality:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@transport@} + {@cost@}n \le {@budget@}} \)
</p>

<p><b>Part (b): Solving the Inequality</b></p>
<p>We solve for \(n\) by isolating the variable. First, we subtract the transport fee from the total budget:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
{@cost@}n &\le {@budget@} - {@transport@} \\
{@cost@}n &\le {@budget - transport@} 
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>Next, we divide by the cost per bag to find the maximum allowed number of bags:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \begin{aligned}
n &\le \frac{{@budget - transport@}}{{@cost@}} \\
n &\le {@disp_raw_n@}\dots
\end{aligned} \)
</p>
<p>Because you cannot buy a fraction of a bag, and buying {@ta_num + 1@} bags would exceed the total budget, we must round <b>down</b> to the nearest whole number.</p>
<p>Hence, the maximum number of whole bags the farmer can buy is:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">
\( \displaystyle \boxed{{@ta_num@}} \)
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
